 shrink as needed, making our programs more flexible and efficient. For example, we can create an array of integers with a specified size, and then use pointers to access and manipulate the elements of the array. This is particularly useful when working with large datasets or when the size of the array is not known in advance.", "highlight": "Dynamic array creation allows for flexible and efficient memory allocation, making it a powerful tool for programming."}, {"type": "example", "title": "Real-World Example", "image_keyword": null, "body": "A real-world example of using arrays and pointers is in the implementation of a grading system. We can create a dynamic array to store the grades of students, and then use pointers to access and manipulate the grades. For example, we can use a pointer to access the grade of a specific student, or we can use pointer arithmetic to calculate the average grade of all students. This makes it easy to manage and analyze large datasets, and is a fundamental concept in programming.", "code": "#include <stdio.h>
